//? Title: Cristian's Algorithm: Reply Timestamps, Delay Intervals and What RTT Cannot Tell Us
//? Subtitle: The original forty-millisecond example, controlled asymmetric paths, known processing and complete probe selection
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A request/reply gives a client a server timestamp and an elapsed round trip,
not the individual network delays. Under an accurate server and ideal-rate
duration model, a common lower bound on each network leg constrains the time
at receipt to an interval. Its midpoint minimizes worst-case absolute error.
We retain the original server reading 1000 ms, round trip 40 ms and minimum
one-way delay 5 ms, then expose controlled exchanges that attain both error
extremes. Native calculations remove known server processing, retain relative
and absolute intervals, and select the first smallest-radius probe after full
validation. Shorter RTT tightens a conditional worst-case bound; it does not
guarantee smaller realized error. Timestamp placement, clock resolution and
the age of a selected observation remain essential parts of the interpretation.
)

\toc

> Start with the timestamp event

Let a be the request's network delay, p the server's known processing duration
and b the reply's network delay. The measured request-to-receipt interval is
R=a+p+b. The server timestamp T_s is taken at reply departure, after p has
elapsed. With an accurate ideal-rate server, its time at client receipt is
T_s+b. All timestamps and durations below use milliseconds consistently.

Remove known processing to obtain δ=R−p=a+b. If both network legs are at
least m≥0, then

\[
m\le b\le\delta-m,\qquad
T_s+m\le T_{\rm receipt}\le T_s+\delta-m.
\]
\alttext(The return delay lies between the common one-way minimum and the network round trip minus that minimum. Adding the reply-departure server timestamp gives the receipt-time interval.)

For the original p=0, R=40, m=5 and T_s=1000, the admissible interval is
[1005,1035], its midpoint is 1020 and its radius is 15. A controlled path
with a=5 and b=35 attains the upper endpoint, so the midpoint error is −15.
The native exchange model supplies both the calculation and the diagram.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cristian_interval.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto x=dm::algorithms::cristian_exchange(1000,5,0,35,5);
  const auto& a=x.analysis;
  if(a.estimate!=1020 || a.error_bound!=15 || a.lower_time!=1005 || a.upper_time!=1035 || x.model_error!=-15) return 1;
  std::print("| server departure stamp | RTT | common minimum | earliest receipt | midpoint estimate | latest receipt | radius | controlled actual receipt | model error |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| 1000 | {} | 5 | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",a.probe.round_trip_time,a.lower_time,a.estimate,a.upper_time,a.error_bound,x.receipt_time,x.model_error);
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(65,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=2.5;box.height=1.25;box.font_size=18;
  box.fill={219,234,254};
  d.node("send",{0,2},"client sends\nrequest",box);
  d.node("receive",{8,2},std::format("client receives\nactual time {}",x.receipt_time),box);
  box.fill={254,243,199};
  d.node("server",{4,-.1},"server replies\nstamp 1000",box);
  d.edge("send","server","request: 5 ms").edge("server","receive","reply: 35 ms");
  box.fill={220,252,231};
  d.node("lower",{0,-3},std::format("earliest\n{}",a.lower_time),box);
  d.node("middle",{4,-3},std::format("estimate\n{}",a.estimate),box);
  d.node("upper",{8,-3},std::format("latest\n{}",a.upper_time),box);
  d.edge("lower","middle","15 ms").edge("middle","upper","15 ms")
   .text({4,4},"Same observed RTT, unknown division between the two paths",22)
   .text({4,-4.8},"Controlled example: actual receipt is the latest allowed time",19)
   .save_svg("build/cristian_interval.svg");
  std::print("\nOriginal helper estimate {}; original helper radius {}.\n",dm::algorithms::cristian_estimate(1000,40),dm::algorithms::cristian_error_bound(40,5));
}
```
