\caption(The controlled asymmetric exchange realizes the original bound. Arrow lengths are conceptual; their duration labels, timestamp and interval endpoints come from the native calculation.)
\alttext(A request arrow takes five milliseconds from client to server, and the reply arrow takes thirty-five back. Three boxes below show earliest time 1005, midpoint 1020 and latest time 1035.)

> Derive the midpoint and its bound

For any candidate return-delay estimate c in [m,δ−m], its worst-case absolute
error is max(c−m,δ−m−c). This is minimized when the two terms are equal,
giving c=δ/2 and radius ε=δ/2−m. The ideal midpoint time and error are

\[
\widehat T=T_s+\frac{R-p}{2},\qquad
\widehat T-(T_s+b)=\frac{a-b}{2},\qquad
|\widehat T-T_{\rm receipt}|\le\frac{R-p}{2}-m.
\]
\alttext(The estimate adds half the network-only round trip to the server stamp. Its ideal error is half the outbound-minus-return delay difference, bounded by half the network round trip minus the common minimum.)

Symmetry a=b makes the corrected midpoint exact in this ideal model. The
interval bound itself does not assume symmetry: it follows from nonnegative
delays and the assumed lower bound. With no defensible positive m, use zero.
The minimum observed in a finite measurement sample is not automatically a
valid universal lower bound on future network delays.

If the server stamp were instead at request arrival, receipt time would be
T_s+p+b and the corrected midpoint would add (R+p)/2. This API deliberately
uses reply-departure stamps, so applying it to arrival stamps without changing
the timestamp convention would create a bias. Ignoring known processing in
the departure-stamp formula adds a bias p/2 even on symmetric network paths.
When processing is unknown and nonnegative, treating it as part of the outbound
leg still gives the conservative uncorrected interval, provided the two network
legs meet the stated minimum; it does not restore symmetry of those combined legs.

> Retain the original RTT sweep

The following independent observations hold m=5 and p=0 fixed, and vary R
from 10 to 100. They are a sensitivity study, not a chronological network
trace. R must be at least 2m: otherwise the assumptions contradict the
observation. At R=2m, both network legs are forced to equal m and the ideal
interval has zero width. Returning zero for R<2m would instead manufacture
false certainty; datamunge now rejects that case.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cristian.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<double> rtts,bounds;
  std::print("| RTT, ms | native ideal radius, ms | receipt-time estimate, ms |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(int r=10;r<=100;r+=10) {
    const auto a=dm::algorithms::cristian_analyze({1000,static_cast<double>(r),5,0});
    if(a.error_bound!=(r-10)/2.) return 1;
    rtts.push_back(r);bounds.push_back(a.error_bound);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n",r,a.error_bound,a.estimate);
  }
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.line(rtts,bounds,"model radius",{37,99,235},2.5)
      .points(rtts,bounds,"",{37,99,235},4)
      .title("Original sensitivity study: a shorter RTT tightens the bound")
      .x_label("observed RTT, milliseconds").y_label("ideal worst-case radius, milliseconds")
      .x_limits(0,105).y_limits(0,50).size(900,560)
      .save_svg("build/cristian.svg");
}
```
