\caption(The original ten radii 0,5,...,45 are preserved, with units and assumptions explicit. These are conditional mathematical widths, not confidence intervals fitted to measured data.)
\alttext(Ten points on a straight line show an ideal error radius increasing by five milliseconds whenever round trip increases by ten, starting at zero radius for a ten-millisecond round trip.)

> Equal RTT does not identify the actual error

Hold R=40, p=0 and m=5. Let b range from 5 to 35, with a=40−b. Every
case produces the same server-based midpoint 1020 and radius 15, yet its
actual ideal error runs from +15 through zero to −15. The native controlled
exchange computes these cases rather than a report-side synchronization loop.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cristian_asymmetry.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<double> returns,errors;
  std::print("| outbound, ms | return, ms | observed RTT, ms | ideal bound, ms | actual model error, ms |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(int b=5;b<=35;++b) {
    const auto x=dm::algorithms::cristian_exchange(1000,40-b,0,b,5);
    if(x.analysis.error_bound!=15 || std::abs(x.model_error)>15) return 1;
    returns.push_back(b);errors.push_back(x.model_error);
    if(b%5==0) std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",x.outbound_delay,x.return_delay,x.analysis.probe.round_trip_time,x.analysis.error_bound,x.model_error);
  }
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.line(returns,errors,"actual model error",{234,88,12},2)
      .abline_h(15,{37,99,235},1.5).abline_h(-15,{37,99,235},1.5)
      .legend({"actual model error","admissible limits +/-15"},{{234,88,12},{37,99,235}})
      .title("Fixed RTT=40 ms: the unknown path split determines the error")
      .x_label("controlled return delay, milliseconds").y_label("estimate minus actual receipt time, milliseconds")
      .x_limits(4,36).y_limits(-18,18).size(900,560)
      .save_svg("build/cristian_asymmetry.svg");
  const auto slow=dm::algorithms::cristian_exchange(1000,20,0,20,5);
  const auto fast=dm::algorithms::cristian_exchange(1000,5,0,15,5);
  if(!(fast.analysis.error_bound<slow.analysis.error_bound && std::abs(fast.model_error)>std::abs(slow.model_error))) return 1;
  std::print("\nSymmetric RTT=40: radius {}, actual error {}. Asymmetric RTT=20: radius {}, actual error {}.\n",slow.analysis.error_bound,slow.model_error,fast.analysis.error_bound,fast.model_error);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| outbound, ms | return, ms | observed RTT, ms | ideal bound, ms | actual model error, ms |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 35 | 5 | 40 | 15 | 15 |
| 30 | 10 | 40 | 15 | 10 |
| 25 | 15 | 40 | 15 | 5 |
| 20 | 20 | 40 | 15 | 0 |
| 15 | 25 | 40 | 15 | -5 |
| 10 | 30 | 40 | 15 | -10 |
| 5 | 35 | 40 | 15 | -15 |

Symmetric RTT=40: radius 15, actual error 0. Asymmetric RTT=20: radius 5, actual error -5.
\image(figures/cristian_asymmetry.svg)
// result_end
\caption(A smaller worst-case interval need not contain a midpoint closer to the actual time than another probe's midpoint. The final printed pair is an explicit counterexample to that stronger inference.)
\alttext(An error line falls from plus fifteen to minus fifteen as return delay rises from five to thirty-five, within constant horizontal bounds at plus and minus fifteen.)

The slower symmetric exchange has zero actual error, whereas the faster
asymmetric exchange has error −5. Its smaller bound is still useful; it
simply answers a worst-case question rather than revealing the hidden path split.

> Known processing, probe choice and timestamp resolution

With equal processing and minimum assumptions across probes, minimizing RTT
also minimizes the radius. If those assumptions vary, minimize the calculated
radius instead. The next independent observations include a 30 ms server
residence duration. Probes 1 and 2 tie at radius zero, and the selection API
retains index 1 as the first minimum, even though probe 2 has the smallest raw
RTT. These different server stamps refer to different receipt instants; choosing
one does not magically advance its old estimate to the present.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto s=dm::algorithms::cristian_select({{1000,20,5,0},{2000,40,5,30},{3000,10,5,0}},3);
  if(!s.has_probe || s.index!=1 || s.probes[1].estimate!=2005) return 1;
  std::print("| probe | raw RTT | known processing | network RTT | minimum | radius | estimate at this probe's receipt |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<s.probes.size();++i) {
    const auto& a=s.probes[i];
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",i,a.probe.round_trip_time,a.probe.server_processing,a.network_round_trip,a.lower_delay,a.error_bound,a.estimate);
  }
  std::print("\nFirst minimum-radius probe index {}. Its timestamp is at its own receipt, not a later present.\n",s.index);
  const auto processed=dm::algorithms::cristian_exchange(1000,20,10,20,5);
  if(processed.analysis.estimate!=1020 || dm::algorithms::cristian_estimate(1000,50)!=1025) return 1;
  std::print("Symmetric 20+20 network paths and 10 processing: corrected {}, uncorrected {}.\n",processed.analysis.estimate,dm::algorithms::cristian_estimate(1000,50));
  const auto large=dm::algorithms::cristian_analyze({1e16,1,0,0});
  if(large.lower_time!=large.upper_time || large.error_bound!=.5 || large.upper_delay!=1) return 1;
  std::print("Rounded epoch endpoints coincide: {}; relative delay interval remains [{},{}], nominal radius {}.\n",large.lower_time==large.upper_time,large.lower_delay,large.upper_delay,large.error_bound);
  bool impossible=false,capped=false,late_invalid=false;
  try { (void)dm::algorithms::cristian_error_bound(8,5); }
  catch(const std::invalid_argument&) { impossible=true; }
  try { (void)dm::algorithms::cristian_select({{0,2,0,0},{0,4,0,0}},1); }
  catch(const std::length_error&) { capped=true; }
  try { (void)dm::algorithms::cristian_select({{0,2,0,0},{0,4,3,0}},1); }
  catch(const std::invalid_argument&) { late_invalid=true; }
  if(!impossible || !capped || !late_invalid) return 1;
  std::print("Impossible minimum rejects; complete cap rejects; invalid later probe validates before cap: pass.\n");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| probe | raw RTT | known processing | network RTT | minimum | radius | estimate at this probe's receipt |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 20 | 0 | 20 | 5 | 5 | 1010 |
| 1 | 40 | 30 | 10 | 5 | 0 | 2005 |
| 2 | 10 | 0 | 10 | 5 | 0 | 3005 |

First minimum-radius probe index 1. Its timestamp is at its own receipt, not a later present.
Symmetric 20+20 network paths and 10 processing: corrected 1020, uncorrected 1025.
Rounded epoch endpoints coincide: true; relative delay interval remains [0,1], nominal radius 0.5.
Impossible minimum rejects; complete cap rejects; invalid later probe validates before cap: pass.
// result_end

The absolute fields use ordinary double rounding, not outward-rounded interval
arithmetic. At epoch 10^16 in the chosen unit, adding a delay between zero and
one can round both endpoints to the same stored timestamp even though the
model interval has width one. Separate delay fields preserve the relevant
sub-epoch quantities here. The output is a nominal calculation, not an
IEEE-754 enclosure certificate. Positive half-durations or radii that collapse
to zero are rejected rather than presented as exact zero uncertainty.

All observations require finite values, nonnegative durations, processing no
larger than RTT and a feasible common delay minimum. Absolute outputs must fit
finite doubles. Controlled exchanges reject path delays below the assumed
minimum and cases where rounding hides positive network duration behind the
processing duration. The original helper signatures and clock_synchronization.hpp
include path remain available, with valid original numbers unchanged.

Selection validates every observation and its derived outputs before the
complete default 100,000-probe cap or allocation. A zero cap disables only
that cap; container/allocation limits remain. It retains all owned analyses,
the first minimum index and an explicit false existence flag for empty input.
It makes two linear passes, using O(n) time/storage. Single-observation
calculations take O(1). The library performs no network I/O or operating-system
clock changes and does not choose between stepping a clock backwards and
slewing its rate. Drift, server clock uncertainty, timestamping overhead and
aging an old probe to a new instant require an extended model; they are not
silently included in this radius.

No external dataset is needed for the original arithmetic and controlled
counterexamples. Native tests cover 27,004 integer path/processing/minimum
combinations, 10,000 exact binary-fraction controls, both extremal errors,
selection ties/ownership, every original sweep row, invalid and contradictory
inputs, complete budgets, underflow/overflow and coarse epoch resolution.
The same focused cases run under address and undefined-behavior sanitizers.

> Reference and scope

F. Cristian (1989), [Probabilistic clock synchronization](https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01784024),
Distributed Computing 3, 146–158; [open paper copy](https://www.cs.cornell.edu/courses/cs5414/2017fa/papers/Cristian.pdf).
The original treatment includes bounded clock drift and a broader probabilistic
time service. This report derives an ideal-rate one-server observation model
and a known-residence correction, not the whole original protocol or an NTP
implementation. Its intervals depend on trustworthy stated assumptions; they
are neither measured confidence intervals nor a claim of perfect physical clocks.
